WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.940
Map the location, orientation, and extent of a rupture.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.700
Measure displaced streams, roads, fences, and rock units to determine movement

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.400
direction and amount. A single-event offset differs from cumulative displacement across

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.600
many earthquakes. Match the same feature across the fault before calculating

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.580
its displacement. Scarps, linear valleys, ridges, ponds, and diverted drainage can

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.080
reveal faults. An isolated linear feature is a clue that needs

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.100
corroboration, not proof by itself.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.760
A trench across a fault exposes displaced sediment and soil layers.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.860
Older layers can preserve several events while younger layers record fewer.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.120
Dated charcoal or peat can bracket deformation.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.240
Date the relevant material and interpret its relationship to the fault;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.400
flowing stream water cannot date the last fault movement.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.720
The North Anatolian example distinguishes a 1.59 m component of 1999

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.060
scarp displacement from a larger cumulative scarp.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.940
Much motion was horizontal, so scarp height alone is not total

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:01.200
slip.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.120
Before-and-after satellite radar measurements can form an interferogram.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.780
Colored fringes represent measured ground displacement along the satellite’s viewing direction;

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.780
they are not rock colors or a direct stress map.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.780
Laser surveying and repeated surface measurements detect deformation.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.040
Instruments in a borehole through an active fault can record conditions

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:27.420
at depth. Combining field relations, dating, and geophysical measurements constrains history

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.500
and recurrence. It does not establish the exact time of the

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.080
next earthquake.
